<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Werden</h1><p class="st-meta">799 CE – 1803 CE (approx.) · capital Werden</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Benedictine abbey on the Ruhr founded by the missionary Saint Ludger, which ruled a small imperial territory for a thousand years.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludger">Saint Ludger</a>, a Frisian missionary and later first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Münster, founded a monastery at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werden">Werden</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> around 799 on land he had acquired. He was buried there in 809, and his family's abbots ran the house for decades. In 877 it became a royal abbey under imperial protection.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werden">Werden</a> held estates from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frisia">Frisia</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>, and its abbots ruled the area around Werden as princes of the Empire. Its library held the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/codex-argenteus">Codex Argenteus</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> Bible of the Gothic language, before it passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uppsala">Uppsala</a>. The protectors of the abbey were the counts of the Mark and later the rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg-prussia">Brandenburg-Prussia</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/werden">Werden</a> remained a Catholic island but a small one. In 1803 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reichsdeputationshauptschluss">Reichsdeputationshauptschluss</a> secularised it and gave it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque</a> church, the basilica of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludger">Saint Ludger</a>, survives in what is now a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/essen">Essen</a>.</p>